updated: '2026-10-13'
---

This walkthrough creates `personal_tool`: a command-line SwiftPM executable, a reusable core library, and an XCTest test. SwiftPM owns the package's builds and dependencies. Xcode handles native Apple application targets, signing, simulators, and platform deployment.

## Check the tools

[Install and load Rivet](/docs/start-here/#install-in-neovim) in Neovim 0.11 or newer. Use a Swift 6.0 or newer toolchain with SwiftPM, and make `swift` available on the editor's `PATH`. The generated manifest starts with `// swift-tools-version: 6.0`.

Check it in a terminal:

```sh
swift --version
swift package --help
```

After creating the package, `:RivetDoctor` checks its Swift tools. Format and Lint use the toolchain's `swift format`, when available. Rivet creates the package using your installed tools; project creation does not install a toolchain or resolve package dependencies.

## Create the package in Neovim

Start in a parent directory of your choice:

```sh
mkdir personal_tool
cd personal_tool
nvim
```

Run `:pwd` to confirm the destination, then `:RivetNew`. Select **New choices** first if a saved-profile menu appears.

| Prompt | Choice for this walkthrough |
| --- | --- |
| Project language | `swift` |
| Create project in | Current folder |
| Project name | `personal_tool` |
| Git repository | Skip Git initialization |
| Project license | No license yet |
| File comment headers | No header |
| Project kind | Application |

Git initialization, a license, and personalized headers are also available. Review the proposed file tree and file contents, then press `c` or Ctrl-S to create the package. Press `q` to cancel the review. The files go directly into `personal_tool`.

The main files are:

```text
personal_tool/
  Package.swift
  .rivet.json
  .gitignore
  .editorconfig
  README.md
  AGENTS.md
  Sources/
    personal_tool/
      main.swift
    personal_toolCore/
      Core.swift
  Tests/
    personal_toolTests/
      CoreTests.swift
```

`Package.swift` declares the executable product, core library, and test target. `Core.swift` exports `sum`; `main.swift` imports the core and prints a result. `.rivet.json` records Rivet's choices and target ownership. SwiftPM creates its `.build/` directory as you build and test.

## Build, run, and test

Open the executable and build it:

```vim
:edit Sources/personal_tool/main.swift
:RivetTask build
```

Select **Debug / development** when prompted. A successful workflow finishes with `Exit status: 0`.

Run `:RivetTask run`. SwiftPM builds and launches `personal_tool`, which prints:

```text
42
```

Run `:RivetTask test`. The XCTest method `CoreTests.testAdd` checks `sum(20, 22) == 42` and `sum(-1, 1) == 0`. Expect one passing XCTest test. Some Swift toolchains print an additional summary for a test runner that has no tests; the XCTest result is the relevant starter result.

Use `q` to close task output. `:RivetPreset` changes Debug/Release, and `:RivetTask repeat` repeats the previous task. With several executable products, `:RivetRunTarget` selects which one Run launches. Check uses the same `swift build` operation as Build.

## Add a source to the core target

Run `:RivetFile`, choose **swift** in the file-language picker, then **Source file** in the **New source file** preview. Check its header sample, or the message that no header is configured. Enter `Answer` as the name and select `personal_toolCore` as the owning target. The review should add `Sources/personal_toolCore/Answer.swift`. Approve with `c` and open it:

```vim
:edit Sources/personal_toolCore/Answer.swift
```

Keep any optional file header and replace the template below it with:

```swift
/// Returns the example answer.
public func answer() -> Int {
    42
}
```

Replace the executable code in `Sources/personal_tool/main.swift` with:

```swift
import personal_toolCore

print("Answer: \(answer())")
```

Save with `:wall`, then run `:RivetTask run` and `:RivetTask test`. Run prints `Answer: 42`, and the original core test still passes.

SwiftPM discovers source files inside its target directories, so this addition preserves `Package.swift`. You can also enter `Sources/personal_toolCore/Answer.swift` as an explicit project-relative path. For customized target directories, choose a path that matches the native manifest; recording a file in Rivet does not add arbitrary paths to a SwiftPM target.

## Follow a compiler error

Temporarily change `answer()` in `main.swift` to `answr()`, save, and run `:RivetTask build`. Swift should report the unknown name.

Run `:copen` and press Enter on the diagnostic to jump to its source location. Use `:cnext` and `:cprevious` for other entries. Correct the spelling, save, and run `:RivetTask repeat`; Build should succeed. Run Test again after making implementation changes.

## Use the CLI instead

With the optional [CLI installed](/docs/getting-started/), start in the parent directory and create the same starter:

```sh
rivet new personal_tool --language swift --kind app --preview
rivet new personal_tool --language swift --kind app
rivet build --root personal_tool
rivet run --root personal_tool
rivet test --root personal_tool
```

The preview creates no files. The second command writes the package, and Run prints `42`. Add the core source with:

```sh
rivet file Answer --language swift --target personal_toolCore --root personal_tool --preview
rivet file Answer --language swift --target personal_toolCore --root personal_tool
```

Apply the source edits from the previous section, then repeat Run and Test. Rivet registers the source with the recorded core target and leaves `Package.swift` intact.

## Document and extend the package

Use Swift documentation comments such as the `///` comment on `answer` for public API descriptions. [Swift-DocC](https://www.swift.org/documentation/docc/) provides Swift documentation tooling; set up its documentation workflow explicitly for your package.

Swift is outside [Doxygen's built-in language list](https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/starting.html#step0). If an existing documentation site requires Doxygen, supply and maintain a suitable external adapter or input filter. Rivet does not generate a Swift-to-Doxygen adapter. See the [Doxygen guide](/docs/doxygen/) for shared documentation and adapter limits.

On macOS with Xcode installed, `:RivetTask xcode` opens `Package.swift` in Xcode. Use the core package as reusable logic when you create an Apple application there. Rivet's starter remains a SwiftPM package.

Continue with [Swift packages and Xcode](/docs/swift/) for additional products and custom native manifests. Use SwiftPM or edit `Package.swift` to add dependencies. `:RivetTarget` can extend an unchanged generated manifest; a customized manifest should be edited through its native workflow.
